livet Cemetery, Bloomfield, New Jersey. Rank and organization: Second Lieutenant, U.S. Army, 115th Infantry, 29th Division. Place and date: At Bois-de-Consenvoye, France; October 8, 1918. Entered service at: Los Angeles, California. Birth: March 25, 1882; Middleborough, Massachusetts. General Orders: War Department, General Orders No. 50\nDocument 7: Patrick M. Regan Patrick M. Regan (born September 1, 1956) is a professor of Political Science and Peace Studies at University of Notre Dame. His research focuses on International Relations and Conflict Management. Regan was born in Dearborn, Michigan and is one of five children born to Michael and Susanne Regan. Regan attended Western Michigan University for his Bachelor's Degree, New York University for his Master's Degree and University of Michigan for his phD. He was also a member of the Peace Corps after receiving his undergraduate degree and travelled to Calcutta to work with Mother Theresa for six weeks.\n\nLook in the other documents for a fact that rules one answer out: a date of birth that makes a person too young, for example, or a plain statement that the answer is false. Use only what these documents say, nothing you know from elsewhere. A fact that only makes an answer less likely does not count.\n\nReply with JSON only. If such a fact is there:\n{\"reason\": \"one sentence\", \"impossible\": \"A\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\"}\n\"impossible\" is the letter of the answer that is ruled out. \"quote\" is the sentence stating the fact, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in.\nIf there is none:\n{\"impossible\": null}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
